---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import FlavorTabs from "../../../components/FlavorTabs.astro";
import ModuleHero from "../../../components/module/ModuleHero.astro";
import ModuleScenario from "../../../components/module/ModuleScenario.astro";
import ModuleFlowDiagram from "../../../components/module/ModuleFlowDiagram.astro";
import AgentLoopDemo from "../../../components/module/demos/AgentLoopDemo.astro";
import ModuleFooterNav from "../../../components/module/ModuleFooterNav.astro";
import ModuleRecap from "../../../components/module/ModuleRecap.astro";
import { flavor as gcp } from "../../../data/flavors/gcp";
import { flavor as aws } from "../../../data/flavors/aws";
import { flavor as azure } from "../../../data/flavors/azure";
import { flavor as oss } from "../../../data/flavors/oss";
import { MODULES } from "../../../data/modules";

const moduleId = "85-agents";
const entries = {
  gcp: gcp[moduleId],
  aws: aws[moduleId],
  azure: azure[moduleId],
  oss: oss[moduleId],
};
const moduleData = MODULES.find((m) => m.id === moduleId)!;
---

<BaseLayout title="85 · Agents & tool use" description={moduleData.summary}>
  <article>
    <ModuleHero moduleId={moduleId}>
      <p>
        Every module so far has been a single retrieval pass answering a single question. Agents are for when the
        task takes more than one step — and after evaluating, observing, securing, and costing out the pipeline, this
        is where you decide when that extra complexity is actually worth it.
      </p>
    </ModuleHero>

    <ModuleScenario moduleId={moduleId} />

    <ModuleFlowDiagram moduleId={moduleId} />

    <h2>Function calling</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Function calling</strong> is the mechanism underneath every agent: the model emits a structured
      description of a tool it wants to call (name and arguments), your runtime executes that tool — often the
      Module 25 retrieval call itself — and the result goes back into the model's context as new input. The model
      never runs code directly; it only ever asks, in a structured format your code chooses whether to honor.
    </p>

    <h2>Agent loops: plan, act, observe</h2>
    <p>
      A single function call answers a single-hop question. An <strong>agent loop</strong> repeats plan → act →
      observe until the task is done: decide what to do next, call a tool, look at the result, and decide again. Left
      unbounded, a loop stuck on an unsolvable task will keep planning and acting forever — so every agent needs a
      hard iteration or token budget that forces it to stop and report failure instead of spinning.
    </p>

    <AgentLoopDemo />

    <h2>MCP as a standard interface</h2>
    <p>
      Early agent frameworks each invented their own way to wire up tools, which meant re-integrating every data
      source per framework. <strong>MCP (Model Context Protocol)</strong> standardizes that interface: a tool or data
      source implements one MCP server, and any MCP-aware agent can call it — the same value a database driver
      provides, applied to agent tooling instead of SQL.
    </p>

    <h2>Agent development kits: buy the loop, don't build it</h2>
    <p>
      The plan-act-observe loop above is a mechanism, not a product — in practice almost nobody hand-rolls it.
      <strong>LangGraph</strong> models the loop as an explicit state graph instead of letting the model decide
      transitions, which is why regulated teams reach for it: deterministic edges, a built-in <code>interrupt()</code>
      for human approval, and a full audit trail. <strong>CrewAI</strong> trades that determinism for speed — role-based
      agents that get a multi-agent prototype running fastest, at the cost of less control once the workflow outgrows
      simple roles. <strong>Google's Agent Development Kit (ADK)</strong> and <strong>Microsoft's Agent Framework /
      Semantic Kernel</strong> are the cloud-native picks when a managed deployment target (Vertex AI Agent Builder,
      Bedrock Agents, Microsoft Foundry Agent Service — the same services named in this module's case study above)
      matters more than framework portability. None of them replace the safety rules below — a framework schedules
      the loop, it doesn't decide what the loop is allowed to touch.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://github.com/jthiruveedula/genai-data-platform/tree/main/labs/85-agents-langgraph-loop" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">A real, runnable lab</a>
      builds the LangGraph pattern above end to end: a genuine <code>StateGraph</code> with a hard iteration budget
      and a human-approval gate on side-effecting tool calls, proven with a test suite that actually runs the loop
      instead of just asserting it compiles.
    </p>

    <h2>Authenticating agents and MCP servers</h2>
    <p>
      An agent's tool calls are only as safe as the credentials behind them. The MCP specification requires any
      remote MCP server to act as an <strong>OAuth 2.1 resource server</strong>: a separate authorization server
      issues the token, the MCP server only validates it and checks scopes — authentication and authorization stay
      two distinct layers, not one. Every tool call over HTTP carries a scoped, short-lived
      <code>Authorization: Bearer</code> token rather than a long-lived API key baked into the agent's config, so a
      leaked token expires and can't be replayed for more than its narrow scope allows.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is least privilege from the safety section below, applied to the credential itself: a research agent's
      token should carry read-only scopes on the one MCP server it needs, never an org-wide service-account key. Per
      cloud, that token minting is Workload Identity Federation (GCP), an IAM role assumed via STS (AWS), or a
      managed identity (Azure) — the OSS flavor's LiteLLM gateway plus an external OAuth provider (Keycloak, Auth0)
      does the same job self-hosted.
    </p>
    <p>
      This isn't just a diagram — <a href="https://github.com/jthiruveedula/genai-data-platform/tree/main/labs/85-agents-mcp-oauth" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">a real, runnable lab</a>
      implements this exact gate: a FastAPI MCP server that mints/validates a scoped JWT and rejects an expired or
      under-scoped token before the tool ever runs, with a test suite that mints real signed tokens to prove it —
      <code>pip install -r requirements.txt && pytest -v</code>, no external identity provider required to try it.
    </p>

    <h2>When RAG beats agents (and vice versa)</h2>
    <p>
      If the task is "look this one thing up," Module 25's single-hop RAG is faster, cheaper, and simpler — an agent
      loop around it just adds latency for no benefit. Agents earn their overhead on genuinely multi-step tasks:
      "find X, then compare it against last quarter's version, then draft a summary" needs planning across several
      tool calls that a single retrieval pass can't do. Knowing which one a request needs is itself a design decision,
      not a default.
    </p>

    <h2>Safety: least privilege and human gates</h2>
    <p>
      An agent should only ever hold the tools and scopes its task actually needs — a research agent doesn't need
      write access to a database. And for any tool call with a real side effect (sending an email, deleting a
      record, spending money), insert a human-approval gate before it executes. Combined with the security lessons
      from Module 65, this is what keeps a tool-using model from turning a planning mistake, or an injected
      instruction, into an action with real consequences.
    </p>

    <h2>Try it in your flavor</h2>
    <FlavorTabs moduleId={moduleId} entries={entries} />

    <h2>Pitfalls</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Running an agent loop with no iteration budget — an unsolvable task will keep planning and acting indefinitely.</li>
      <li>Reaching for an agent on a single-hop lookup that Module 25's plain RAG API already answers faster and cheaper.</li>
      <li>Granting an agent broad tool access "for flexibility," which turns any planning mistake or injected instruction into a much larger blast radius.</li>
      <li>Letting an agent execute side-effecting tool calls (sending, deleting, spending) with no human approval step in between.</li>
      <li>Picking a framework by hype instead of fit — CrewAI's speed on a workflow that needed LangGraph's audit trail, or vice versa.</li>
      <li>Baking a long-lived API key into an agent's config instead of a scoped, short-lived OAuth token an MCP server can validate per call.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
  <ModuleRecap moduleId={moduleId} />
  <ModuleFooterNav moduleId={moduleId} />
</BaseLayout>
